{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c3e3","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a1832d-22a7-7bd3-b4f8-0a937add9938"},"sequence":76,"recorded_at":1780531400000147,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"01a1832d-2557-7c22-bf4d-f6336f6f7b2d","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"command_id":"01a1832d-2557-7c22-bf4d-f6336f6f7b2d","event":{"chunk":"docs/contracts/narration.md:65:the Modal L4 rate (`KOKORO_L4_USD_PER_SEC`, about USD 0.80/h). The\nagent-logs/40-impl.jsonl:5:{\"schema_version\":1,\"id\":\"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c355\",\"stream\":{\"kind\":\"session\",\"id\":\"01a182fd-1b82-7092-96c0-ebd4e6f80f13\"},\"sequence\":5,\"recorded_at\":1780531400000005,\"record_type\":\"status\",\"durability\":\"ephemeral\",\"causation_id\":\"01a182fd-1d24-7451-81eb-d015551c1a5a\",\"payload_type\":\"turn.input.user\",\"payload_schema_version\":1,\"payload\":{\"command_id\":\"01a182fd-1d24-7451-81eb-d015551c1a5a\",\"kind\":\"turn_input_user\",\"prompt\":\"# Task 40: chapter narration with Kokoro on Modal (pipeline, cache, contract)\\n\\nRepo martinmw13/LectorIA-meta, worktree /workspace/wt-40, branch `narration` (checked out, from main). Read AGENTS.md first and follow every repo rule (co-author trailer via the hook, the 5000-token file-size hook, check:secrets, core stays pure, server files start with `import \\\"server-only\\\"`). `package.json` already has `modal@0.11.1` (the Modal JS SDK) installed but uncommitted: commit it with your work. Do NOT run the real pipeline, do NOT call Modal, Meta, Jev, Blob, Redis or any paid API, do NOT run `modal deploy`, do NOT edit anything under data/. Do not run `next start` or `next dev`. Tests use fakes only. Do not touch app/ (the reader UI is a later task).\\n\\n## Goal\\nRead-aloud narration, voice Kokoro-82M (Apache-2.0) `af_heart`, speed 0.95, generated on a Modal GPU function that scales to zero. The unit is the CHAPTER (owner decision): one compact mp3 per chapter plus a per-chapter manifest with sentence timings, so the reader can play any scene by seeking to its first sentence and can highlight the sentence being read. One reusable function, `ensureChapterNarration`, is used offline for Oz now and later on demand for uploaded books. Every chapter's audio lives under a content-addressed cache key, so a second reader of the same book (or the same Oz text) gets it free.\\n\\n## 1. `core/narration.ts` (pure, no env, no network, no node: imports) + tests\\n- Constants: `NARRATION_VOICE = \\\"af_heart\\\"`, `NARRATION_MODEL = \\\"kokoro-82m/0.9.4\\\"`, `NARRATION_SPEED = 0.95`, `NARRATION_FORMAT = 1`, `SENTENCE_GAP_SEC = 0.3`, `PARAGRAPH_GAP_SEC = 0.7`.\\n- `splitSentences(text: string): { start: number; end: number }[]`: char offsets into the paragraph text, trimmed of surrounding whitespace, covering every non-space char. A sentence ends at `.`, `!` or `?` (also `…`), plus any closing quotes/brackets (`”’\\\"')]`), followed by whitespace. Do not end after common abbreviations (Mr. Mrs. Dr. St. Mt. etc. — keep a small list) or a single capital initial. Merge a piece shorter than 3 words into the previous sentence (or the next one if first). Test with real Oz-style text containing curly quotes, em dashes, \\\"Mr.\\\", \\\"!” said Dorothy\\\", and a quote ending a paragraph.\\n- `NarrationChunk = { n: number; start: number; end: number; text: string }` and `chapterChunks(paragraphs: { n: number; text: string }[]): { chunks: NarrationChunk[]; gaps: number[] }`. `gaps[i]` is the silence after chunk i: `PARAGRAPH_GAP_SEC` when the next chunk is a new paragraph, `SENTENCE_GAP_SEC` otherwise, 0 after the last.\\n- `narrationKeyInput({ chunks, voice, model, speed, format }): string`: a canonical string built from the NFC-normalized, whitespace-collapsed chunk texts (joined with \\\"\\\\n\\\"), voice, model, speed and format. Callers hash it with SHA-256 (core takes no crypto); `narrationKey(hashHex: string, voice: string): string` returns `narration/v${NARRATION_FORMAT}/${voice}/${hashHex.slice(0, 32)}`. Audio path is `${key}.mp3`, manifest path `${key}.json`. Same text + voice + model + speed => same key, any edit => new key (test it).\\n- `ChapterNarration = { format: 1; key: string; src: string; durationSec: number; voice: string; model: string; speed: number; chunks: { n: number; start: number; end: number; t0: number; t1: number }[] }` and a strict `parseChapterNarration(json: unknown): ChapterNarration` that throws on any bad field (it is a trust boundary: the reader fetches it). `src` must be an `https:` URL or a same-origin path starting with `/narration/`. Times finite, `0 <= t0 < t1 <= durationSec + 0.05`, chunks sorted by t0, n integers, start < end.\\n- `activeChunkAt(chunks, tSec): number`: binary search, index of the last chunk with `t0 <= t` (so the highlight stays on a sentence through the gap after it), -1 before the first chunk.\\n- `sceneSpan(narration, startN, endN): { startSec: number; endSec: number } | null`: from t0 of the first chunk with `n >= startN` to t1 of the last chunk with `n <= endN`; null when no chunk falls in the range.\\n- `chaptersToPrepare(event: \\\"upload\\\" | \\\"enter\\\" | \\\"jump\\\", ch: number, totalChapters: number): number[]` (owner's rolling window): upload => [1, 2]; enter chapter N => [N+1, N+2]; jump to N => [N, N+1]. Clip to 1..total, dedupe, keep order.\\n- Cost: `KOKORO_L4_USD_PER_SEC = 0.000222` (Modal L4, about USD 0.80/h) and `estimateNarrationMicro(chars: number): number` = ceil((chars / 15 * 0.02 + 8) * KOKORO_L4_USD_PER_SEC * 1e6): about 15 chars of audio per second, GPU time about 2% of audio after encode, plus 8 s of cold start/overhead. `actualNarrationMicro(gpuSec)` = ceil(gpuSec * rate * 1e6). Integer micro-dollars, as the repo rules require.\\n- `ensureChapterNarration(input: { chunks: NarrationChunk[]; gaps: number[]; voice?: string; speed?: number }, deps: NarrationDeps): Promise<EnsureResult>` with\\n  ```ts\\n  interface NarrationDeps {\\n    sha256Hex(text: string): Promise<string>;\\n    index: { get(key: string): Promise<ChapterNarration | null>; set(key: string, value: ChapterNarration): Promise<void> };\\n    store: { getManifest(key: string): Promise<ChapterNarration | null>; putAudio(key: string, mp3: Uint8Array): Promise<string>; putManifest(key: string, value: ChapterNarration): Promise<void> };\\n    lock?: { acquire(key: string): Promise<string | null>; release(key: string, token: string): Promise<void> };\\n    budget?: { reserve(micro: number): Promise<boolean>; refund(micro: number): Promise<void> };\\n    synthesize(req: { texts: string[]; gaps: number[]; voice: string; speed: number }): Promise<{ mp3: Uint8Array; durationSec: number; spans: { t0: number; t1: number }[]; gpuSec: number }>;\\n  }\\n  type EnsureResult =\\n    | { status: \\\"ready\\\"; narration: ChapterNarration; cached: boolean; costMicro: number }\\n    | { status: \\\"pending\\\" }\\n    | { status: \\\"error\\\"; code: \\\"budget\\\" | \\\"failed\\\" };\\n  ```\\n  Flow, in this order: key => `index.get` hit => ready cached, cost 0, no lock, no budget. Miss => `store.getManifest` hit => `index.set` backfill, ready cached. Miss => `lock.acquire` (null => pending). Then re-check the index once (another holder may have just finished). Then `budget.reserve(estimateNarrationMicro(totalChars))` (false => release, error budget). Then synthesize (validate `spans.length === texts.length`, else failed), putAudio, build and validate the manifest with `parseChapterNarration`, putManifest, index.set, release, return ready with `cached: false` and `costMicro` = the reserved estimate (the cap counts what was reserved; never refund on success). Any throw after the reserve => refund once, release, error failed. Release failures are swallowed (the lock has a TTL). Never put error text in the result. Spend is counted only when audio is generated, never on hits. Tests with in-memory fakes: hit from index, hit from store with backfill, concurrent second caller gets pending while the first holds the lock, budget refusal releases the lock and never synthesizes, synthesize/store failure refunds exactly once and releases, success counts the estimate, a second call after success is a free hit, and the manifest timings round-trip through `sceneSpan` and `activeChunkAt`.\\nSplit into `core/narration.ts` and `core/narration-cache.ts` (or similar) if a file would pass the size hook; tests likewise.\\n\\n## 2. Modal function `pipeline/modal/narrate.py`\\nReuse this proven eval setup (it ran fine):\\n```python\\nkimg=(modal.Image.debian_slim(python_version=\\\"3.11\\\").apt_install(\\\"espeak-ng\\\",\\\"ffmpeg\\\").pip_install(\\\"kokoro==0.9.4\\\",\\\"soundfile\\\",\\\"numpy\\\",\\\"torch==2.5.1\\\",\\\"transformers==4.46.3\\\"))\\nvol=modal.Volume.from_name(\\\"lectoria-hf-cache\\\",create_if_missing=True)  # mounted at /root/.cache/huggingface\\n# KPipeline(lang_code=\\\"a\\\"); for _,_,audio in pipe(text, voice=voice, speed=speed): ... 24 kHz float32\\n```\\nApp name `lectoria-narration`, one function `narrate(texts: list[str], gaps: list[float], voice: str, speed: float) -> dict` with `gpu=\\\"L4\\\"`, `scaledown_window=60` (scale to zero after a minute idle), `timeout=600`, `max_containers=4`. Load the KPipeline once per container (module global or `@modal.enter` on a class; a plain function with a lazily created global is fine). Validate inputs (1..2000 texts, each 1..2000 chars, gaps same length and 0..3 s, voice matches `^[a-z]{2}_[a-z]+$`, 0.5 <= speed <= 1.5). Synthesize each text separately, concatenating all audio the pipeline yields for it, append `gaps[i]` seconds of silence, and record `t0`/`t1` in seconds from exact sample counts. Encode the whole chapter with ffmpeg to mp3 mono 24 kHz 32 kbps CBR (`-ac 1 -ar 24000 -b:a 32k -codec:a libmp3lame`). Return `{ \\\"mp3\\\": bytes, \\\"durationSec\\\": float, \\\"spans\\\": [{\\\"t0\\\",\\\"t1\\\"}...], \\\"gpuSec\\\": float }` where gpuSec is wall time inside the function. No secrets in the file. Clean the text lightly before TTS only (it never changes the offsets): turn `—` into `, ` when between words, drop `_` emphasis marks.\\n\\n## 3. `pipeline/kokoro.ts` (Node, reads env)\\n`createKokoroClient()` returns the `synthesize` dep: `new ModalClient()` (it reads MODAL_TOKEN_ID / MODAL_TOKEN_SECRET from env itself; never read, log or pass them), `await modal.functions.fromName(\\\"lectoria-narration\\\", \\\"narrate\\\")`, `fn.remote([], { texts, gaps, voice, speed })`. Validate the response shape strictly (mp3 is a Uint8Array/Buffer, spans length matches, numbers finite) and throw a short generic error otherwise. One retry on a thrown call. Errors must never include tokens or headers.\\n\\n## 4. Pipeline step `narrate` (`pipeline/narrate.ts`, `pipeline/freshness.ts`, `pipeline/build.ts`)\\n- Add `narrate` as the last step in `STEPS`, `FreshStep` and `STEP_ORDER`, with `narrateInputOf(textVersion, voice, model, speed, format)` and `narrateKeys(dir)` reading `provenance.input` of `data/books/<book>/narration.json`. Extend freshness tests.\\n- `runNarrate(ctx, { synthesize, store })`: for every chapter in book.json, `chapterChunks` over that chapter's paragraphs, then `ensureChapterNarration` with: `sha256Hex` from node:crypto; `index` backed by the previous narration.json (`chapters[ch]` whose `key` matches); `store` = Blob when `BLOB_READ_WRITE_TOKEN` is set (`@vercel/blob` `put` with `addRandomSuffix: false`, `allowOverwrite: false`, content type audio/mpeg or application/json, long cache; manifest lookup via `list({ prefix })` exact pathname then fetch, like server/images/blob.ts), else a local store writing `public/narration/v1/...` (audio `src` becomes `/narration/v1/<voice>/<hash>.mp3`, manifest json next to it). Same keys either way, so switching to Blob later is a re-run. No lock. `budget` = an in-process cap: `NARRATION_CAP_USD` env (default 2, invalid => default), reserve refuses past the cap so the run stops and reports instead of spending more. Run chapters with concurrency 4. Write `data/books/<book>/narration.json` = `{ textVersion, provenance: { input, voice, model, speed, format, chapters_generated, chapters_cached, gpu_seconds, cost_usd_estimated, cost_usd_actual }, chapters: { \\\"<ch>\\\": ChapterNarration } }` (cost_usd_actual from `actualNarrationMicro(gpuSec)` summed). Print one line per chapter (`ch 3: generated 412.5 s audio, 7.1 s gpu` or `ch 3: cached`) and a cost summary.\\n- `build.ts`: `--step narrate` creates the Kokoro client lazily only when the step runs; `--dry-run` covers it. Narration needs neither META_API_KEY nor JEV_API_KEY.\\n- Tests for runNarrate with a fake synthesize and a temp dir: writes manifests for every chapter, second run is all cached with zero cost, cap refusal stops spending.\\n\\n## 5. Server cache adapters `server/narration/cache.ts` (no route yet)\\n`import \\\"server-only\\\"`. `createNarrationDeps({ kv, env, synthesize })` returning NarrationDeps for the future upload route: index in Redis under `narr:idx:<key>` (JSON, validated with parseChapterNarration on read), lock `narr:lock:<key>` via `kv.setNxEx(…, token, 300)` and `kv.releaseIfToken`, Blob store as above (server version), budget reserving on BOTH the global spend key and the daily spend key from core/images (`spendKey()`, `dailySpendKey(day)`) against `env.spendCapMicro` / `env.dailySpendCapMicro`, refunding both if either cap is passed (same pattern as server/images/orchestrate.ts). A BYOK path is not part of narration (narration always uses our Modal credits). Plus `server/narration/kokoro.ts` = `import \\\"server-only\\\"; export { createKokoroClient } from \\\"@/pipeline/kokoro\\\";` if that type-checks and builds; otherwise say so. Test the budget and lock adapters with `createMemoryKV()`.\\n\\n## 6. Contract `docs/contracts/narration.md`\\nDocument: chapter unit and why; the manifest shape and `sceneSpan`; cache key (hash of normalized chapter text + voice + model + speed + format), Blob paths, Redis index and lock (TTL 300 s, dedupes concurrent requests so a second reader gets it free); spend accounting (reserve the estimate on both caps only when generating, refund on failure, cache hits are free, global DAILY_SPEND_CAP_USD applies); the owner's rolling window for uploads (at upload chapters 1 and 2 while the book is processed; entering chapter N ensures N+1 and N+2; jumping to N ensures N and N+1; the client asks, the server dedupes); cold start (about 15–30 s for the first chapter after the function scaled to zero, warm calls a few seconds per chapter; the 2-ahead window hides it after chapter 1; while a chapter is pending the Read aloud control shows a quiet \\\"Getting the voice ready…\\\" state); cost per chapter (formula plus a worked number: an average Oz chapter of ~9,000 chars ≈ 10 min of audio ≈ USD 0.003 warm); Oz is pregenerated through the same keys; Modal is deployed with `modal deploy pipeline/modal/narrate.py` and scales to zero, and `modal app stop lectoria-narration` removes it entirely; open items (upload route wiring, Blob vs public/ for Oz). Link it from docs/contracts/book-data.md in one line. Also write docs/tasks/40-narration.md (short design: what and why) and add a BUILT_DURING_HACKATHON.md row in the existing format. Add `NARRATION_CAP_USD` to `.env.example` (name only).\\n\\n## Done when\\n`npm test`, `npm run typecheck`, `npm run check:size`, `npm run check:secrets`, `npm run test:hooks` pass, and `npx tsx pipeline/build.ts oz --step narrate --dry-run` prints `narrate: redo (no previous output)`. `python3 -m py_compile pipeline/modal/narrate.py` passes. Commit in a few focused commits (imperative subjects under 72 chars, bodies say why). Do not push. End with a short summary of files and anything you could not do.\\n\",\"run_stream\":{\"id\":\"01a182fd-1d24-7451-81eb-d015551c1a5a\",\"kind\":\"run\"}}}\nagent-logs/40-impl.jsonl:6:{\"schema_ve